eq x \\leq 1, \\, 0 \\leq y \\leq x^3\\big\\}$" 13194139534778 17592186045417 true] [17592186192764 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435741 13194139534778 17592186045417 true] [17592186192765 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435740 13194139534778 17592186045417 true] [17592186192767 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435739 13194139534778 17592186045417 true] [17592186192924 1050 420.0 13194139534778 17592186045417 true] [17592186192944 1071 "Which of the following sets are closed?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 4,\\,1\\le y\\le 4}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<5}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le x+1,\\,-1\\le x\\le 1}$" 13194139534778 17592186045417 false] [17592186192944 1071 "Which of the following sets are closed?\n\nI. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 4,\\,1\\le y\\le 4\\}$\n\nII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<5\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le x+1,\\,-1\\le x\\le 1\\}$" 13194139534778 17592186045417 true] [17592186193322 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534778 17592186045417 true] [17592186193322 1071 "Which of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\n1. ${(x, y) ∈ R^{2}: (x + 1)^{2} + (y - 1)^{2} < 4}$\n2. ${(x, y) ∈ R^{2}: 1 \\le x^{2} \\le 9, 0 \\le y \\le 1}$\n3. ${(x, y) ∈ R^{2}: 0 \\le x \\le 2, 1 < y < 4}$" 13194139534778 17592186045417 false] [17592186193322 1071 "Which of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\nI. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}<4\\}$\n\nII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }1\\le {x}^{2}\\le 9,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }0\\le x\\le 2,\\,1<y<4\\}$" 13194139534778 17592186045417 true] [17592186193322 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second is *closed* since it contains all its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The third is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(0,2).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534778 17592186045417 true] [17592186241002 1071 "Which of the following sets are closed?\n\n- $[4,8]\\cup [10,12]$\n- $[0,5)$\n- $[1,3)\\cup (3,7]$" 13194139534778 17592186045417 false] [17592186241002 1071 "Which of the following sets are closed?\n\nI. $[4,8]\\cup [10,12]$\n\nII. $[0,5)$\n\nIII. $[1,3)\\cup (3,7]$" 13194139534778 17592186045417 true] [17592186278577 1071 "Which of the following sets are closed?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-3<x<-1,\\,1<y<0.5x+3}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 4-{x}^{2}}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1<x<2,\\,0<y<2}$" 13194139534778 17592186045417 false] [17592186278577 1071 "Which of the following sets are closed?\n\nI. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-3<x<-1,\\,1<y<0.5x+3\\}$\n\nII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 4-{x}^{2}\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1<x<2,\\,0<y<2\\}$" 13194139534778 17592186045417 true] [17592186278578 1071 "Which of the following sets are closed?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1<x<2,\\,0<y<\\frac{1}{x}}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-1<x<1,\\,0<y<\\sqrt{1-x}}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 8,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{x}}$" 13194139534778 17592186045417 false] [17592186278578 1071 "Which of the following sets are closed?\n\nI. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1<x<2,\\,0<y<\\frac{1}{x}\\}$\n\nII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-1<x<1,\\,0<y<\\sqrt{1-x}\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{